pattern: reject invalid UTF-8 instead of returning an uncompilable expression

Regexp documents that a nil error means the result can be passed to
regexp.MustCompile, and the expand package does exactly that. For a
pattern holding invalid UTF-8, such as one read from a Latin-1 script,
Regexp returned the bytes unchanged, regexp.Compile rejected them, and
expansion panicked. Go regular expressions cannot match invalid UTF-8,
so Regexp now reports it as an error.

This also adds fuzz targets: Regexp results must compile and stay
proportional to the pattern, BracesSeq must stop within its element
limit, and Format must not panic.

Reproducer, before this change: regexp.MustCompile(Regexp("\xff", 0))
panicked with "error parsing regexp: invalid UTF-8".

